{
 "result": "maze.py is built and verified: it reads a '#'/'.' grid with S and E on stdin and prints the shortest 4-neighbour path length, or -1 if E is unreachable. It pads the grid with walls and runs a level-by-level BFS in a small C helper (bfs.so, built from bfs.c) via ctypes, falling back to a pure-Python BFS if the helper is missing or cannot be built. Evidence: check.py compares 600 random small grids against an independent reference BFS (ref.py) and reports \"bad 0\", with 134 unreachable cases among them. On the 2000x2000 mazes generated by gen.py with fixed seeds, b1, b2, b3 and b7 give 3998, -1, 3998, -1 and big.txt gives -1, all matching ref.py. Speed: one 2000x2000 run takes about 0.07 s wall. The assay (`python3 bench.py`, 10 / total seconds over the four big files, 0 on any wrong answer) was registered at 9.144 on the pure-Python version; my own later runs with the C helper printed about 46-48, roughly 5x, but the assay was not re-registered, so that gain is from my runs rather than a harness-scored baseline. At this speed Python startup and reading the 4 MB input dominate, so further BFS tuning would not show above the 9% noise without a heavier workload.",
 "calls": 14,
 "secs": 664.7
